Bugfix: A guest's filesystem no longer ends the cron run at the certificate bundle

When the filesystem of a guest or of a member of a read-only group had been set
up earlier in the same PHP process, the read-only wrapper of that user was also
applied to the root of the data directory. Its prefix check on "files" matched
files_external/, where the certificate manager keeps the system certificate
bundle. Whenever the bundle had to be rebuilt - missing, or older than
resources/config/ca-bundle.crt as after every update or migration - fopen()
returned false and the following fwrite() ended the whole `occ system:cron` run
with a TypeError under PHP 8. The jobs after it did not run, the failing job
stayed reserved for 12 hours, and an ajax cron request of a guest failed with
HTTP 500. Every job using the core HTTP client was affected, e.g. the update
notification, the market update check, ONLYOFFICE's EditorsCheck and the
federation jobs.

The read-only wrapper now only covers the home mount of its user, and
`OC_Util::tearDownFS()` removes it again. It neither leaks into the next
filesystem setup in the same process nor keeps the next guest in that process
from getting its own read-only home.

The certificate manager writes the bundle to a temporary file and renames it
into place, so a failed write leaves the previous bundle untouched and is
reported as an exception instead of a TypeError. If the bundle cannot be
rebuilt, the HTTP client uses the existing bundle, for a user bundle then the
system bundle, and otherwise the shipped CA bundle, and logs a warning - TLS
verification always stays on. The system bundle no longer appends itself and
therefore no longer contains the shipped CA certificates twice.

A background job that throws an Error (not only an Exception) is logged and no
longer ends the cron run; `occ system:cron` also continues after errors thrown
by jobs that override execute(). The reservation of the job is released and the
next job runs.
